# OpenWrt Repository Maintenance Guide

## Purpose

This document describes the recommended workflow for maintaining the RDS OpenWrt
fork and custom packages while remaining synchronized with upstream OpenWrt
feeds and repositories.

This workflow avoids modifying stock feed packages directly and instead keeps
customizations in a separate feed repository.

## Branch Structure

The OpenWrt fork uses the following branch structure:

```
openwrt-25.12   upstream tracking branch
rds-25.12       RDS firmware development branch
```

Rules:

* openwrt-25.12 must always track upstream OpenWrt with no custom changes.
* rds-25.12 contains RDS-specific firmware changes.
* All package customizations should be stored in the RDS feed repository.

## Feed Repository

A separate repository stores all custom packages and overridden upstream packages.

Example repository:

```
https://github.com/<youruser>/rds-feed
```

Typical structure:

```
rds-feed/
    net/
    utils/
    admin/
    luci/
```

Each package directory contains a normal OpenWrt package definition.

## Adding the Custom Feed

Add the custom feed to OpenWrt by editing feeds.conf or feeds.conf.default:

```
src-git rds https://github.com/<youruser>/rds-feed.git
```

Then update feeds:

```
./scripts/feeds update -a
```

## Importing a Stock Package for Customization

Stock packages must never be modified directly inside the feeds directory.

Instead copy the package into the custom feed repository.

Example:

```
cp -a feeds/packages/net/tcpdump ~/rds-feed/net/
```

Commit the imported package:

```
cd ~/rds-feed
git add net/tcpdump
git commit -m "Import tcpdump from OpenWrt packages feed"
git push
```

After this, modifications should be made only inside the rds-feed repository.

## Installing a Custom Feed Package

After modifying a package in the custom feed repository:

Update the feed in the OpenWrt tree:

```
./scripts/feeds update rds
```

Install the package from the custom feed:

```
./scripts/feeds install -f -p rds <packagename>
```

Example:

```
./scripts/feeds install -f -p rds tcpdump
```

Options explained:

```
-f    force reinstall if package already installed
-p    specify the feed name
```

## Daily Build Workflow

Typical workflow before building firmware:

```
./scripts/feeds update -a
./scripts/feeds install -a
```

Then reinstall overridden packages from the RDS feed:

```
./scripts/feeds install -f -p rds <package list>
```

Example:

```
./scripts/feeds install -f -p rds tcpdump mwan3 luci-app-mwan3
```

## Updating Upstream Feeds

Stock feeds can be safely updated using:

```
./scripts/feeds update -a
./scripts/feeds install -a
```

Since customizations are stored in the rds-feed repository,
updating feeds will not overwrite local modifications.

After updating feeds, reinstall overridden packages:

```
./scripts/feeds install -f -p rds <packagename>
```

## Syncing Custom Packages with Upstream

When upstream updates a package that has been overridden:

1. Update upstream feeds.

   ```
   ./scripts/feeds update packages
   ```

2. Compare the upstream version with the custom version.

   ```
   diff -ru feeds/packages/net/tcpdump ~/rds-feed/net/tcpdump
   ```

3. Merge desired upstream changes into the rds-feed package.

4. Commit the updated package.

   ```
   cd ~/rds-feed
   git add net/tcpdump
   git commit -m "tcpdump: merge upstream package updates"
   git push
   ```

5. Reinstall the updated package in the OpenWrt tree.

   ```
   ./scripts/feeds update rds
   ./scripts/feeds install -f -p rds tcpdump
   ```

## Rules for Package Customization

Rule 1

Never edit packages inside:

```
feeds/packages/
feeds/luci/
feeds/telephony/
```

These directories are controlled by upstream feeds.

Rule 2

All custom or modified packages must live in the rds-feed repository.

Rule 3

After running:

```
./scripts/feeds install -a
```

any overridden packages must be reinstalled explicitly:

```
./scripts/feeds install -f -p rds <packagename>
```

Rule 4

Core OpenWrt modifications belong in the OpenWrt fork,
not in the feed repository.

Examples of core modifications:

```
target/
include/
package/base-files/
kernel configuration
board definitions
firmware image generation
```

## Updating the OpenWrt Fork

To update the upstream OpenWrt branch:

```
git checkout openwrt-25.12
git pull --rebase upstream openwrt-25.12
```

Then update the RDS development branch:

```
git checkout rds-25.12
git rebase openwrt-25.12
```

Push the updated branch:

```
git push --force-with-lease
```

## Summary

OpenWrt fork (this repository):

```
tracks upstream OpenWrt
contains firmware build changes
contains board and system modifications
```

RDS feed repository:

```
contains custom packages
contains overridden upstream packages
maintains package history independently
```

This structure allows safe upstream updates while preserving
all RDS customizations under version control.
